WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.232
Ask a large language model a question,

00:00:02.232 --> 00:00:03.946
and watch the answer arrive.

00:00:03.946 --> 00:00:05.237
It does not appear.

00:00:05.237 --> 00:00:06.105
It trickles.

00:00:06.305 --> 00:00:07.968
One word, then the next, then the

00:00:07.968 --> 00:00:09.722
next, at a pace that has almost

00:00:09.722 --> 00:00:11.570
nothing to do with how much compute

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:12.206
you own.

00:00:12.406 --> 00:00:14.468
Here is the part that surprises people.

00:00:14.468 --> 00:00:17.642
During that trickle, your very expensive accelerator

00:00:17.642 --> 00:00:19.907
is sitting almost completely idle.

00:00:20.107 --> 00:00:22.094
To produce a single token, the model

00:00:22.094 --> 00:00:23.789
has to read every one of its

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:25.126
weights out of memory.

00:00:25.126 --> 00:00:27.867
For a seventy billion parameter model, that

00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:30.419
is well over a hundred gigabytes moved,

00:00:30.419 --> 00:00:31.191
for one word.

00:00:31.391 --> 00:00:34.845
The arithmetic itself is trivial by comparison.

00:00:34.845 --> 00:00:36.775
The chip finishes the math in a

00:00:36.775 --> 00:00:38.609
small fraction of the time, and then

00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:39.274
it waits.

00:00:39.274 --> 00:00:41.108
And then it waits again, for the

00:00:41.108 --> 00:00:43.060
next word, and the one after that,

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.012
for as long as the answer runs.

00:00:45.213 --> 00:00:46.910
Which leads somewhere strange.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:49.062
Reading those weights costs the same whether

00:00:49.062 --> 00:00:51.214
you use them to compute one token

00:00:51.214 --> 00:00:52.433
or a hundred.

00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:54.716
The bottleneck is the trip, not the

00:00:54.716 --> 00:00:55.457
cargo.

00:00:55.657 --> 00:00:57.680
That asymmetry is the whole trick.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.994
Generating a word is slow, because each

00:00:59.994 --> 00:01:02.017
one depends on the one before it.

00:01:02.217 --> 00:01:04.191
Checking a word is fast, because a

00:01:04.191 --> 00:01:06.257
whole batch of them can be examined

00:01:06.257 --> 00:01:08.024
in a single pass, using weights you

00:01:08.024 --> 00:01:09.698
were going to load anyway.

00:01:09.898 --> 00:01:11.125
So here is the move.

00:01:11.125 --> 00:01:13.537
Instead of asking the expensive model to

00:01:13.537 --> 00:01:15.257
write the answer, we are going to

00:01:15.257 --> 00:01:17.267
ask it to grade one that somebody

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:18.494
else already wrote.

00:01:18.694 --> 00:01:20.874
Alongside the large model, we run a

00:01:20.874 --> 00:01:21.514
small one.

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:24.127
Maybe twenty times smaller, and far quicker.

00:01:24.327 --> 00:01:26.515
Most of what any model writes is

00:01:26.515 --> 00:01:27.850
not actually hard.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.936
Articles, prepositions, the second half of a

00:01:30.936 --> 00:01:31.833
common phrase.

00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:33.802
The small model gets those right almost

00:01:33.802 --> 00:01:34.918
every time.

00:01:35.118 --> 00:01:37.017
So it runs ahead and drafts the

00:01:37.017 --> 00:01:39.414
next handful of tokens by itself.

00:01:39.414 --> 00:01:41.811
Five words, guessed cheaply, in the time

00:01:41.811 --> 00:01:43.710
the large model would have spent on

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:44.683
part of one.

00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:46.782
Now the large model reads all five

00:01:46.782 --> 00:01:49.417
drafted tokens in a single forward pass.

00:01:49.417 --> 00:01:51.102
One trip to memory.

00:01:51.102 --> 00:01:52.787
One set of weights loaded.

00:01:52.787 --> 00:01:55.422
Five predictions checked in parallel.

00:01:55.622 --> 00:01:57.899
It compares each drafted token against what

00:01:57.899 --> 00:01:59.436
it would have chosen itself.

00:01:59.636 --> 00:02:01.936
Everything from the beginning of the draft

00:02:01.936 --> 00:02:04.236
up to the first disagreement is accepted

00:02:04.236 --> 00:02:05.511
and kept, in one go.

00:02:05.711 --> 00:02:08.106
At that first disagreement, the large model

00:02:08.106 --> 00:02:10.500
supplies the correct token itself, drawn from

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.217
a carefully corrected distribution.

00:02:13.217 --> 00:02:15.611
Nothing is quietly dropped, and nothing is

00:02:15.611 --> 00:02:17.498
silently approximated.

00:02:17.698 --> 00:02:20.659
That is what makes speculative decoding unusual

00:02:20.659 --> 00:02:21.665
among speed-ups.

00:02:21.866 --> 00:02:23.592
The text you get out is sampled

00:02:23.592 --> 00:02:26.181
from exactly the same distribution as the

00:02:26.181 --> 00:02:26.850
slow path.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.479
Bit for bit, it is the same

00:02:28.479 --> 00:02:29.148
model.

00:02:29.348 --> 00:02:31.867
In practice, on ordinary prose, the draft

00:02:31.867 --> 00:02:34.574
is accepted often enough to deliver two

00:02:34.574 --> 00:02:36.261
to three times the throughput.

00:02:36.261 --> 00:02:38.989
No retraining, no quantisation, no loss of

00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:39.842
quality.

00:02:39.842 --> 00:02:42.341
You are simply using the memory bandwidth

00:02:42.341 --> 00:02:44.027
you were already paying for.

00:02:44.227 --> 00:02:46.775
On dense or unpredictable text, more of

00:02:46.775 --> 00:02:49.081
the draft gets rejected, and the gain

00:02:49.081 --> 00:02:50.554
shrinks toward nothing.

00:02:50.554 --> 00:02:52.728
The speed-up is really a bet on

00:02:52.728 --> 00:02:54.925
how predictable your output is.

00:02:55.125 --> 00:02:57.396
Which is the lesson hiding underneath all

00:02:57.396 --> 00:02:58.036
of it.

00:02:58.036 --> 00:03:00.328
The wait was never the arithmetic.

00:03:00.328 --> 00:03:02.207
It was the walk to memory.

00:03:02.207 --> 00:03:03.765
And once you see that, the fix

00:03:03.765 --> 00:03:05.531
stops looking like a trick, and starts

00:03:05.531 --> 00:03:08.349
looking obvious: make every walk carry more.
